#: manual/design/cli.md:34
msgid "Pure helpers, native effects"
msgstr "純粋なヘルパーと native の副作用"

#: manual/design/cli.md:36
msgid "*Problem.* Process execution and asynchronous standard streams exist only on the native target. *Choice.* Parsing, help texts, JSONL replay extraction and report rendering are ordinary functions in `cli.mbt`; the native `main.mbt` is async and uses `moonbitlang/async` for stdin, stdout and processes; other targets get `main_unimplemented.mbt`, which supports file-to-file `report` and rejects `replay`. *Why.* The same package builds on every target, and the logic is tested without spawning anything."
msgstr "*問題。* プロセス実行と非同期の標準ストリームは native ターゲットにしか存在しません。*選択。* 解析、ヘルプテキスト、JSONL からのリプレイ情報の抽出、レポートの描画は `cli.mbt` の通常の関数です。native の `main.mbt` は async で、stdin、stdout、プロセスに `moonbitlang/async` を使います。その他のターゲットは `main_unimplemented.mbt` を使い、ファイルからファイルへの `report` をサポートし、`replay` を拒否します。*理由。* 同じパッケージがすべてのターゲットでビルドでき、ロジックは何も起動せずにテストできます。"

#: manual/design/cli.md:44
msgid "Guarded replay"
msgstr "保護されたリプレイ"

#: manual/design/cli.md:46
msgid "*Problem.* A replay artifact contains a command line; executing it blindly from a file found in an issue would run arbitrary code. *Choice.* `replay` requires a file (no stdin), prints the command with `--dry-run`, executes only with `--yes`, enforces the recorded timeout, and fails on a non-zero exit. *Why.* The safe default is to look; executing is an explicit second step."
msgstr "*問題。* リプレイのアーティファクトにはコマンドラインが含まれます。issue で見つけたファイルからそれを無条件に実行すれば、任意のコードが実行されてしまいます。*選択。* `replay` はファイルを必須とし（stdin は不可）、`--dry-run` でコマンドを表示し、`--yes` があるときだけ実行し、記録されたタイムアウトを適用し、非ゼロの終了で失敗します。*理由。* 安全な既定は確認することであり、実行は明示的な第 2 段階です。"

#: manual/design/cli.md:52
msgid "Reading only what is needed"
msgstr "必要なものだけを読む"

#: manual/design/cli.md:54
msgid "`replay_spec_from_jsonl` stops at the first `validation_failure` and reads only the three replay fields, after checking the version of every line before it. A stream with many events and one failure works, and an artifact from an unknown version is rejected before anything runs."
msgstr "`replay_spec_from_jsonl` は最初の `validation_failure` で止まり、それより前の各行のバージョンを検査した後、3 つのリプレイ用フィールドだけを読みます。多数のイベントと 1 つの失敗を含むストリームでも動作し、未知のバージョンのアーティファクトは何かが実行される前に拒否されます。"

#: manual/design/cli.md:59
msgid "Portable reports"
msgstr "ポータブルなレポート"

#: manual/design/cli.md:61
msgid "`report` reads the whole input, builds the document with the target label `native`, and writes one HTML file. `-` for input or output connects it to pipes, so it composes with other tools."
msgstr "`report` は入力全体を読み込み、ターゲットラベル `native` でドキュメントを構築し、1 つの HTML ファイルを書き出します。入力や出力に `-` を指定するとパイプにつながるため、他のツールと組み合わせられます。"

#: manual/design/cli.md:65
msgid "Decisions on the terminal"
msgstr "Decisions on the terminal"

#: manual/design/cli.md:67
#, fuzzy
msgid "*Problem.* In CI the HTML is an artifact someone has to download, but the question \"did it get slower?\" should be answered in the job log. *Choice.* After writing the report, `report` prints the comparison table of `@report.comparisons_text` on stdout. When the HTML itself goes to stdout (`-`), the table goes to stderr, so a pipeline still receives a clean HTML document; `--quiet` suppresses it with the progress lines. `--baseline <id>` chooses the implementation the others are compared with. *Why.* The table is computed by `report` from the record, so the terminal and the HTML show the same decisions; the CLI only decides where the text goes. A baseline that names no implementation of the record exits with `1`, not `2`: the command line is well formed, but it does not fit the data, like an input file with invalid events. A `--baseline` without a value is a usage error and exits with `2`."
msgstr "*Problem.* In CI the HTML is an artifact someone has to download, but the question \"did it get slower?\" should be answered in the job log. *Choice.* After writing the report, `report` prints the comparison table of `@report.comparisons_text` on stdout. When the HTML itself goes to stdout (`-`), the table goes to stderr, so a pipeline still receives a clean HTML document; `--quiet` suppresses it with the progress lines. `--baseline <id>` chooses the implementation the others are compared with. *Why.* The table is computed by `report` from the record, so the terminal and the HTML show the same decisions; the CLI only decides where the text goes. A baseline that names no implementation of the record exits with `1`, not `2`: the command line is well formed, but it does not fit the data, like an input file with invalid events. A `--baseline` without a value is a usage error and exits with `2`."

