#: manual/design/model.md:105
msgid "Run identity: recoverable components"
msgstr "Run identity: recoverable components"

#: manual/design/model.md:107
#, fuzzy
msgid "`run_identity` joins five components with `|`: the case id, the protocol identity, the decimal seed, the provenance run id and the provenance timestamp. The free-text components pass through the escape map $\\epsilon$ that replaces `%` by `%25` and then `|` by `%7C`. Its output contains no `|`, and neither do the protocol identity and the decimal seed, so splitting a run id on `|` gives exactly five parts. $\\epsilon$ is injective: replacing `%7C` by `|` and then `%25` by `%` inverts it, because every `%` in the output starts one of the two escapes, so `%7C` matches only escaped bars. Hence two run ids are equal exactly when all five components are equal, and runs that differ in seed, provenance run id or timestamp never share an id."
msgstr "`run_identity` joins five components with `|`: the case id, the protocol identity, the decimal seed, the provenance run id and the provenance timestamp. The free-text components pass through the escape map $\\epsilon$ that replaces `%` by `%25` and then `|` by `%7C`. Its output contains no `|`, and neither do the protocol identity and the decimal seed, so splitting a run id on `|` gives exactly five parts. $\\epsilon$ is injective: replacing `%7C` by `|` and then `%25` by `%` inverts it, because every `%` in the output starts one of the two escapes, so `%7C` matches only escaped bars. Hence two run ids are equal exactly when all five components are equal, and runs that differ in seed, provenance run id or timestamp never share an id."

#: manual/design/model.md:120
msgid "Versioned identifiers"
msgstr "バージョン付きの識別子"

#: manual/design/model.md:122
#, fuzzy
msgid "*Problem.* Readers of old JSONL must not silently misread new fields. *Choice.* Three version enums produce the identifiers `mmkp_<n>` (protocol identity), `mmka_1` (event artifacts) and `mmks_2` (Plot IR). Every event carries `artifact_version`; readers reject unknown versions. *Why.* Additive changes keep the version; a breaking change adds a version and a lifecycle entry, and old readers fail loudly instead of guessing. The protocol identity went through exactly that: `mmkp_1:<w>:<c>:<d>` covered only the warmup count, the confirmatory sample count and the threshold, so different protocols shared a key. `ProtocolVersion::V2` (`mmkp_2`) covers every field, and `V1` is `Deprecated`: it names old keys and is no longer produced. A key states its version, so an `mmkp_1` key can never be mistaken for an `mmkp_2` one."
msgstr "3 つのバージョン enum は `mmkp_1`（protocol の語彙）、`mmka_1`（event artifact）、`mmks_2`（Plot IR）の識別子を生成します。すべてのイベントに `artifact_version` があり、reader は未知のバージョンを拒否します。追加的な変更ではバージョンを維持し、互換性を壊す変更では `V2` とライフサイクル項目を追加します。旧 reader は推測せず、明確に失敗します。"

#: manual/design/model.md:134
msgid "Three-part environment snapshots"
msgstr "3 つの部分からなる環境スナップショット"

#: manual/design/model.md:136
#, fuzzy
msgid "*Problem.* Some differences invalidate correctness (target, flags, ABI), some invalidate timings (CPU, GC, clock, frequency policy), and some are just bookkeeping (hostname, time, revision). *Choice.* `SemanticEnvironment`, `PerformanceEnvironment` and `ProvenanceEnvironment`, with compatibility on the first two. *Why.* Two runs on different hosts with the same declared hardware remain comparable; a run with different flags does not. `model` itself does not probe the machine: a snapshot is a value, built by hand or by the separate [`env_detect`](env_detect.md) package, which fills it from the running process and lists what it could not observe. Keeping the probe out of `model` keeps the vocabulary free of IO and lets tests build exact snapshots."
msgstr "*問題。* 違いには、正しさを無効にするもの（ターゲット、フラグ、ABI）、計時を無効にするもの（CPU、GC、時計、周波数ポリシー）、単なる記録上のもの（ホスト名、時刻、リビジョン）があります。*選択。* `SemanticEnvironment`、`PerformanceEnvironment`、`ProvenanceEnvironment` に分け、互換性は最初の 2 つで判定します。*理由。* 宣言されたハードウェアが同じなら、異なるホストでの 2 つの実行は比較可能なままです。フラグが異なる実行は比較できません。スナップショットは宣言したものを記録します。mare_mark はマシンを調査しません。"

#: manual/design/model.md:147
msgid "Explicit protocols"
msgstr "明示的なプロトコル"

#: manual/design/model.md:149
#, fuzzy
msgid "*Problem.* \"Ran the benchmark\" hides the experimental design, warmup, batch sizes, order, sample counts, validation coverage and the decision threshold. *Choice.* `RunProtocol` names all of them, `protocol_canonical_encoding` writes every field, and `protocol_identity` digests that text into a short key (derived above). The runner stores the full protocol and the seed in the `RunSummary`, and the JSONL summary carries both. *Why.* A result is reproducible only if its protocol is, and a key that ignores a field lets two different experiments look like one. A digest keeps the key short enough for file names and tables while the record keeps the fields themselves."
msgstr "*Problem.* \"Ran the benchmark\" hides the experimental design, warmup, batch sizes, order, sample counts, validation coverage and the decision threshold. *Choice.* `RunProtocol` names all of them, `protocol_canonical_encoding` writes every field, and `protocol_identity` digests that text into a short key (derived above). The runner stores the full protocol and the seed in the `RunSummary`, and the JSONL summary carries both. *Why.* A result is reproducible only if its protocol is, and a key that ignores a field lets two different experiments look like one. A digest keeps the key short enough for file names and tables while the record keeps the fields themselves."

#: manual/design/model.md:159
msgid "Run ids that name one execution"
msgstr "Run ids that name one execution"

#: manual/design/model.md:161
#, fuzzy
msgid "*Problem.* A run id built from the protocol and the case alone is the same for every repetition of a nightly job. *Choice.* `run_identity` adds the run seed and the provenance run id and timestamp, escaped so that the parts stay recoverable (derived above). *Why.* The id then distinguishes executions, and a reader can still see from the id which case and protocol a run used."
msgstr "*Problem.* A run id built from the protocol and the case alone is the same for every repetition of a nightly job. *Choice.* `run_identity` adds the run seed and the provenance run id and timestamp, escaped so that the parts stay recoverable (derived above). *Why.* The id then distinguishes executions, and a reader can still see from the id which case and protocol a run used."

