#: manual/design/runner.md:359
#, fuzzy
msgid "Long-lived setups (`PerRun`, `PerDataset`, `PerImplementation`) are prepared once per dataset and implementation and cached across that dataset's warmup, calibration and blocks. They are reset after warmup and after calibration, after every measurement validation under `EveryMeasurement`, and at the end of the dataset."
msgstr "長寿命のセットアップ（`PerRun`、`PerDataset`、`PerImplementation`）は、実装とデータセットごとに一度準備され、ウォームアップ後とキャリブレーション後にリセットされ、データセットの終わりに再びリセットされます。ランナーは実装ごとに 1 つのキャッシュを持つため、現在のところ `PerRun` と `PerDataset` は `PerImplementation` と同様に振る舞います。"

#: manual/design/runner.md:365
#, fuzzy
msgid "*Problem.* The names suggest three lifetimes: one value for the whole run, one per dataset, one per implementation. *Options.* Share a prepared value across datasets; share it across implementations; key it by (dataset, implementation). *Choice.* The cache is keyed by (dataset, implementation), and `PerRun` is rejected when the run has more than one dataset. *Why.* A prepared value is $p = \\operatorname{prepare}(\\operatorname{clone}(x_d), i)$, a function of the input $x_d$ of dataset $d$ and of the implementation $i$. Dataset $d'$ has another input $x_{d'}$; running its blocks on $p$ would measure the workload of $x_d$ while recording $d'$, so every observation of $d'$ would be mislabelled, and under `EveryMeasurement` it would be validated against the expected results of $x_{d'}$. The value must therefore be rebuilt for every dataset, which is what `PerDataset` says. Sharing across implementations is excluded by the API: `prepare` receives the implementation id precisely so that it may build a different layout per implementation. With both sharings excluded, `PerDataset` and `PerImplementation` describe the same cache, and `PerRun` is that cache when the run has exactly one dataset. With more than one dataset a run-wide value would have to come from one dataset's input, so `BenchSpec::compile` (counting scales, or the datasets of a given protocol) and `run` reject it with `PerRunSetupWithMultipleDatasets` instead of silently preparing per dataset."
msgstr "*Problem.* The names suggest three lifetimes: one value for the whole run, one per dataset, one per implementation. *Options.* Share a prepared value across datasets; share it across implementations; key it by (dataset, implementation). *Choice.* The cache is keyed by (dataset, implementation), and `PerRun` is rejected when the run has more than one dataset. *Why.* A prepared value is $p = \\operatorname{prepare}(\\operatorname{clone}(x_d), i)$, a function of the input $x_d$ of dataset $d$ and of the implementation $i$. Dataset $d'$ has another input $x_{d'}$; running its blocks on $p$ would measure the workload of $x_d$ while recording $d'$, so every observation of $d'$ would be mislabelled, and under `EveryMeasurement` it would be validated against the expected results of $x_{d'}$. The value must therefore be rebuilt for every dataset, which is what `PerDataset` says. Sharing across implementations is excluded by the API: `prepare` receives the implementation id precisely so that it may build a different layout per implementation. With both sharings excluded, `PerDataset` and `PerImplementation` describe the same cache, and `PerRun` is that cache when the run has exactly one dataset. With more than one dataset a run-wide value would have to come from one dataset's input, so `BenchSpec::compile` (counting scales, or the datasets of a given protocol) and `run` reject it with `PerRunSetupWithMultipleDatasets` instead of silently preparing per dataset."

#: manual/design/runner.md:386
msgid "Warmup and calibration once per scale"
msgstr "Warmup and calibration once per scale"

#: manual/design/runner.md:388
#, fuzzy
msgid "On the first dataset of a scale every implementation runs single-iteration batches until it has run `warmup_iterations` batches and spent `warmup_time_us`, capped at $\\max(\\text{warmup\\_iterations}, \\text{max\\_batch\\_iterations})$ batches, and is then calibrated. The same warmup applies to every implementation, so none starts the measurement with an advantage from just-in-time compilation or caches. Later datasets of the scale reuse the calibrated batch sizes: datasets of one scale have the same size and, by construction of the generator, the same distribution, so the cost per iteration and with it the right batch size is the same up to noise. Repeating warmup and calibration per dataset would multiply their cost by $D$ and make the batch size, and with it the timer error, differ between datasets of the same scale."
msgstr "On the first dataset of a scale every implementation runs single-iteration batches until it has run `warmup_iterations` batches and spent `warmup_time_us`, capped at $\\max(\\text{warmup\\_iterations}, \\text{max\\_batch\\_iterations})$ batches, and is then calibrated. The same warmup applies to every implementation, so none starts the measurement with an advantage from just-in-time compilation or caches. Later datasets of the scale reuse the calibrated batch sizes: datasets of one scale have the same size and, by construction of the generator, the same distribution, so the cost per iteration and with it the right batch size is the same up to noise. Repeating warmup and calibration per dataset would multiply their cost by $D$ and make the batch size, and with it the timer error, differ between datasets of the same scale."

#: manual/design/runner.md:401
msgid "Crash isolation through subprocess workers"
msgstr "サブプロセスワーカーによるクラッシュの隔離"

#: manual/design/runner.md:403
msgid "*Problem.* A candidate that segfaults or loops forever would end the experiment. *Choice.* `Implementation::worker` runs each operation as a child process inside a task group with a hard-cancel handler, captures stdout and stderr concurrently, and turns a timeout or non-zero exit into an outcome. *Why.* An outcome is data: it is counted, reported and replayable. Process creation is part of the measured operation for workers, which is acceptable for correctness corpora and unsafe code, not for micro-benchmarks."
msgstr "*問題。* セグメンテーション違反を起こしたり無限ループしたりする候補は、実験を終わらせてしまいます。*選択。* `Implementation::worker` は各操作を、強制キャンセルのハンドラを持つタスクグループ内の子プロセスとして実行し、stdout と stderr を並行してキャプチャし、タイムアウトや非ゼロの終了を結果に変換します。*理由。* 結果はデータであり、数えられ、報告され、リプレイできます。ワーカーではプロセスの生成が計測される操作の一部になりますが、これは正しさのコーパスや安全でないコードには許容できても、マイクロベンチマークには適しません。"

